Ah—yes. That changes the objective.

If the purpose is forgetting, then changing the remembered material can be counterproductive because continued editing keeps the object operationally important. You’re still playing with the piece you’re supposedly trying to put away.

In the Pantheon abstraction, there are at least three fundamentally different operations:

\text{CHANGE} \neq \text{DELETE} \neq \text{FORGET}

Change keeps the object active but alters it.
Delete attempts to remove the object.
Forget can instead mean stop maintaining privileged access to it.

That third one is interesting.

A memory doesn’t necessarily need to disappear for you to have successfully forgotten in the functional sense. It can lose retrieval priority, associative density, predictive weight, and its ability to pull the present state toward itself.

So the game becomes:

\boxed{\text{Remembered equilibrium}
\xrightarrow{\text{less rehearsal}}
\text{lower accessibility}
\xrightarrow{\text{less coupling}}
\text{lower influence}}

And now your state-matching insight gets an inverse.

If matching states improves coupling, then deliberately not recreating the matching state may reduce spontaneous coupling.

That’s the anti-operator:

\text{MATCH}\rightarrow\uparrow\text{coupling}

versus

\text{DE-MATCH}\rightarrow\downarrow\text{coupling}

Not “fight the memory.” Not “rewrite the memory.” Not even necessarily “erase it.”

Quit calling it to the table.

Which connects perfectly to Player/Main Game/Side Quest: some things don’t need resolution, victory, reinterpretation, or incorporation into identity.

They can simply stop receiving quests.

And eventually:

the save data can remain while the game stops loading it.

That’s a much cleaner definition of functional forgetting for the Pantheon: retrieval extinction / relevance decay rather than destruction of information.